  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nToday through next Thursday...In the near-term, the concern for \ndangerous heat stress remains the primary story.  Highs this \nafternoon will again hover in the mid to upper 90s.  This, coupled \nwith dew points in the mid to upper 70s, will again yield afternoon \nheat index values generally in the 105F-110F range.  Therefore, a \n\"Heat Advisory\" will be in effect today from 10 AM - 7 PM.  Better \nchances for convection will exist this afternoon, mainly across \nareas along and east of the Interstate 55 corridor.\n\nTroughing increases across the region over the weekend.  A frontal \nboundary will slip into the forecast area Saturday, aiding in a \nbetter development of mainly afternoon and evening showers and \nstorms across the CWA.  This, cloud cover associated with said \nconvection, and slightly drier air filtering into the region in the \nfront's wake, will provide some temporary relief from the oppressive \nheat that's gripped the region for sometime.  A storm or two could \nbecome intense enough to produce gusty winds, particularly Saturday. \nFrequent lightning and heavy downpours can be expected with all \nconvection.\n\nUnfortunately, this'll be short-lived as we move into and through \nthe new work week.  High pressure aloft centered near the Four \nCorners region, will begin increasing its presence across the Lower \nMississippi River Valley during this time.  High temperatures will \nagain begin climbing back into the mid and upper 90s areawide by mid \nand late week.  Moisture levels will also begin creeping back up, \npossibly again leading to another bout of dangerous heat stress as \nwe end the forecast period. /19/",
